    sampleAnswer: "The copy assignment operator (operator=(const T&)) defines how an already-constructed object is assigned the contents of another object of the same type, as opposed to a copy constructor, which builds a brand-new object from an existing one. The compiler implicitly generates a member-wise copy assignment operator if the class doesn't declare one, doesn't have a user-declared move operation, and all its members are themselves copy-assignable. This implicit version is dangerous for classes that manage a resource directly (a raw pointer to allocated memory, a file handle) because it performs a shallow copy, leading to double-free or use-after-free bugs - which is exactly the problem the Rule of Three/Five addresses.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe copy assignment operator (operator=(const T&)) defines how an already-constructed object is assigned the contents of another object of the same type, as opposed to a copy constructor, which builds a brand-new object from an existing one. The compiler implicitly generates a member-wise copy assignment operator if the class doesn't declare one, doesn't have a user-declared move operation, and all its members are themselves copy-assignable. This implicit version is dangerous for classes that manage a resource directly (a raw pointer to allocated memory, a file handle) because it performs a shallow copy, leading to double-free or use-after-free bugs - which is exactly the problem the Rule of Three/Five addresses.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nclass Buffer {\n    int* data;\n    size_t size;\npublic:\n    Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other) {\n        if (this == &other) return *this;  // guard against self-assignment\n        delete[] data;\n        size = other.size;\n        data = new int[size];\n        std::copy(other.data, other.data + size, data);\n        return *this;\n    }\n};\n```",
