    sampleAnswer: "Const correctness means using const consistently to express which data isn't meant to change - const parameters, const member functions, and const references - so the compiler can catch accidental mutation at compile time rather than it becoming a runtime bug. A const member function promises not to modify the object's observable state (technically, the implicit this pointer becomes a pointer-to-const inside that function), which matters because it's the only way to call a member function on a const object or through a const reference; the compiler enforces this by rejecting non-const member calls or member modification inside a function marked const, except for members explicitly marked mutable.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nConst correctness means using const consistently to express which data isn't meant to change - const parameters, const member functions, and const references - so the compiler can catch accidental mutation at compile time rather than it becoming a runtime bug. A const member function promises not to modify the object's observable state (technically, the implicit this pointer becomes a pointer-to-const inside that function), which matters because it's the only way to call a member function on a const object or through a const reference; the compiler enforces this by rejecting non-const member calls or member modification inside a function marked const, except for members explicitly marked mutable.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nclass Account {\n    double balance;\npublic:\n    double getBalance() const { return balance; }  // can be called on a const Account&\n    void deposit(double amt) { balance += amt; }    // cannot be called on a const Account&\n};\nvoid printBalance(const Account& acc) {\n    std::cout << acc.getBalance();  // OK; acc.deposit(10) here would fail to compile\n}\n```",
