    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA by-value parameter receives its own independent copy of the argument, so modifications inside the function don't affect the caller's object, at the cost of a copy (which can be expensive for large objects). A reference parameter (T&) aliases the caller's actual object, so the function can read and modify it directly without copying, and const T& gives the efficiency of avoiding a copy while still preventing the function from modifying the caller's object - which is why const T& is the conventional choice for passing large objects that shouldn't be modified.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nvoid byValue(std::vector<int> v) { v.push_back(1); }         // caller's vector unaffected\nvoid byRef(std::vector<int>& v) { v.push_back(1); }          // modifies caller's vector\nvoid byConstRef(const std::vector<int>& v) { /* read v, no copy, no mutation allowed */ }\n```",
