    shortDescription: "Both allocate raw, uninitialized-by-default heap memory and return a void*, but malloc(size) allocates a single block of the requested size with unspecified (garbage) contents, whi...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because memory ownership and object lifetime are central to writing safe, reliable C++ systems.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.\n- They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.\n- They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it.",
    sampleAnswer: "Both allocate raw, uninitialized-by-default heap memory and return a void*, but malloc(size) allocates a single block of the requested size with unspecified (garbage) contents, while calloc(num, size) allocates space for num elements of size bytes each and explicitly zero-initializes the entire block. calloc is the natural choice when you specifically need zeroed memory (like a fresh array of counters) without a separate memset call, and its two-argument form also helps guard against a multiplication overflow that a manual num * size passed to malloc could silently produce.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBoth allocate raw, uninitialized-by-default heap memory and return a void*, but malloc(size) allocates a single block of the requested size with unspecified (garbage) contents, while calloc(num, size) allocates space for num elements of size bytes each and explicitly zero-initializes the entire block. calloc is the natural choice when you specifically need zeroed memory (like a fresh array of counters) without a separate memset call, and its two-argument form also helps guard against a multiplication overflow that a manual num * size passed to malloc could silently produce.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nint* a = (int*)malloc(10 * sizeof(int));   // 10 ints, indeterminate values\nint* b = (int*)calloc(10, sizeof(int));    // 10 ints, all zero-initialized\nfree(a); free(b);\n```",
