    shortDescription: "malloc/free are C library functions that only allocate/release raw memory; they know nothing about C++ objects and never call constructors or destructors. new/delete are C++ operat...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because memory ownership and object lifetime are central to writing safe, reliable C++ systems.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.\n- They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.\n- They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it.",
    sampleAnswer: "malloc/free are C library functions that only allocate/release raw memory; they know nothing about C++ objects and never call constructors or destructors. new/delete are C++ operators: new allocates memory and then invokes the object's constructor, and delete invokes the destructor and then releases the memory. Mixing them is undefined behavior: calling free() on memory obtained from new skips the destructor entirely (leaking any resources the object owned, like heap memory or file handles), and calling delete on memory obtained from malloc can crash or corrupt the heap, because the two allocators may use entirely different internal bookkeeping and delete additionally attempts to run a destructor on memory that was never properly constructed as that C++ type.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nmalloc/free are C library functions that only allocate/release raw memory; they know nothing about C++ objects and never call constructors or destructors. new/delete are C++ operators: new allocates memory and then invokes the object's constructor, and delete invokes the destructor and then releases the memory. Mixing them is undefined behavior: calling free() on memory obtained from new skips the destructor entirely (leaking any resources the object owned, like heap memory or file handles), and calling delete on memory obtained from malloc can crash or corrupt the heap, because the two allocators may use entirely different internal bookkeeping and delete additionally attempts to run a destructor on memory that was never properly constructed as that C++ type.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nMyClass* a = new MyClass();  // allocates memory AND calls the constructor\ndelete a;                     // calls the destructor AND deallocates\n\nMyClass* b = (MyClass*)malloc(sizeof(MyClass));  // allocates raw memory only\n// b's constructor never ran - using b as a MyClass object here is already unsafe\nfree(b);  // correct pairing for malloc, but the object was never properly constructed\n```",
