    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because memory ownership and object lifetime are central to writing safe, reliable C++ systems.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.\n- They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.\n- They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it.",
    sampleAnswer: "Both are undefined behavior, not just bad style. delete on malloc'd memory will attempt to invoke a destructor on memory that was never constructed as that type, and its deallocation logic assumes the C++ runtime's own allocator bookkeeping, which malloc's implementation may lay out completely differently - this can crash immediately, corrupt the heap silently, or appear to work by luck depending on the platform and allocator, which makes it a particularly dangerous class of bug. free on new'd memory never runs the destructor, so any resources the object's destructor would have released (further heap allocations, file handles, locks) leak, and it similarly risks heap corruption from mismatched allocator bookkeeping.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBoth are undefined behavior, not just bad style. delete on malloc'd memory will attempt to invoke a destructor on memory that was never constructed as that type, and its deallocation logic assumes the C++ runtime's own allocator bookkeeping, which malloc's implementation may lay out completely differently - this can crash immediately, corrupt the heap silently, or appear to work by luck depending on the platform and allocator, which makes it a particularly dangerous class of bug. free on new'd memory never runs the destructor, so any resources the object's destructor would have released (further heap allocations, file handles, locks) leak, and it similarly risks heap corruption from mismatched allocator bookkeeping.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Both of the following are undefined behavior and should never appear in real code:\nint* a = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int));\ndelete a;      // WRONG: delete expects new's allocation bookkeeping\n\nint* b = new int(5);\nfree(b);       // WRONG: skips no-op destructor here, but wrong for class types generally\n               // and still mismatches new's allocator bookkeeping\n```",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.", "They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.", "They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it."],
