    shortDescription: "Virtual memory is an abstraction provided by the operating system and CPU's memory management unit that gives each process its own private, contiguous-looking address space, which...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because memory ownership and object lifetime are central to writing safe, reliable C++ systems.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.\n- They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.\n- They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it.",
    sampleAnswer: "Virtual memory is an abstraction provided by the operating system and CPU's memory management unit that gives each process its own private, contiguous-looking address space, which the OS maps to physical RAM (or disk-backed swap/page files) transparently via page tables. This is why a program can be compiled and linked assuming fixed addresses without knowing what else is running on the machine, why one process can't directly read another's memory, and why a program can appear to use more memory than the machine physically has installed, with the OS paging less-used memory out to disk as needed. Within a C++ process's virtual address space, memory is conventionally organized into segments - the stack (function call frames, grows and shrinks automatically), the heap (dynamic allocations via new/malloc, must be explicitly managed), and static/global storage and the code segment (fixed at load time).",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nVirtual memory is an abstraction provided by the operating system and CPU's memory management unit that gives each process its own private, contiguous-looking address space, which the OS maps to physical RAM (or disk-backed swap/page files) transparently via page tables. This is why a program can be compiled and linked assuming fixed addresses without knowing what else is running on the machine, why one process can't directly read another's memory, and why a program can appear to use more memory than the machine physically has installed, with the OS paging less-used memory out to disk as needed. Within a C++ process's virtual address space, memory is conventionally organized into segments - the stack (function call frames, grows and shrinks automatically), the heap (dynamic allocations via new/malloc, must be explicitly managed), and static/global storage and the code segment (fixed at load time).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Two separate running instances of the same program can each believe their\n// heap-allocated pointer lives at, say, address 0x0000... - virtual memory maps\n// each process's identical-looking virtual addresses to different physical RAM.\n```",
