    shortDescription: "RAII ties a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime: acquiring the resource (memory, a file handle, a mutex lock, a socket) happens in the constructor, and releasing it happens...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because memory ownership and object lifetime are central to writing safe, reliable C++ systems.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to distinguish allocation, construction, ownership, and destruction correctly.\n- They want you to identify leaks, dangling objects, mismatched allocation, or unnecessary ownership when relevant.\n- They want you to prefer an appropriate RAII or smart-pointer design when the problem calls for it.",
    sampleAnswer: "RAII ties a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime: acquiring the resource (memory, a file handle, a mutex lock, a socket) happens in the constructor, and releasing it happens in the destructor, which C++ guarantees runs automatically when the object goes out of scope - including when an exception unwinds the stack. This makes resource cleanup deterministic and exception-safe without needing manual cleanup code at every possible exit point (early returns, exceptions, breaks), which is exactly the problem manual malloc/free or lock/unlock pairing is prone to getting wrong. std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr, std::lock_guard, and std::fstream are all standard-library RAII wrappers built on this idea.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nRAII ties a resource's lifetime to an object's lifetime: acquiring the resource (memory, a file handle, a mutex lock, a socket) happens in the constructor, and releasing it happens in the destructor, which C++ guarantees runs automatically when the object goes out of scope - including when an exception unwinds the stack. This makes resource cleanup deterministic and exception-safe without needing manual cleanup code at every possible exit point (early returns, exceptions, breaks), which is exactly the problem manual malloc/free or lock/unlock pairing is prone to getting wrong. std::unique_ptr, std::shared_ptr, std::lock_guard, and std::fstream are all standard-library RAII wrappers built on this idea.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nvoid process() {\n    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);  // mutex acquired here\n    doWorkThatMightThrow();                  // if this throws...\n}  // ...the mutex is still released automatically as 'lock' is destroyed during unwinding\n```",
