    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "If a base class is deleted through a base-class pointer without a virtual destructor, only the base class's destructor runs, not the derived class's - leaving any resources the der...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "If a base class is deleted through a base-class pointer without a virtual destructor, only the base class's destructor runs, not the derived class's - leaving any resources the derived part of the object owns (heap memory, file handles) leaked, and technically making the deletion undefined behavior according to the standard. Declaring the base destructor virtual ensures that deleting through a base pointer correctly runs the full destructor chain, starting with the most-derived class's destructor and proceeding up to the base. This only matters for classes actually intended to be used polymorphically through base pointers/references; a class not designed for inheritance and polymorphic deletion doesn't need a virtual destructor and paying for the vtable overhead would be unnecessary.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nIf a base class is deleted through a base-class pointer without a virtual destructor, only the base class's destructor runs, not the derived class's - leaving any resources the derived part of the object owns (heap memory, file handles) leaked, and technically making the deletion undefined behavior according to the standard. Declaring the base destructor virtual ensures that deleting through a base pointer correctly runs the full destructor chain, starting with the most-derived class's destructor and proceeding up to the base. This only matters for classes actually intended to be used polymorphically through base pointers/references; a class not designed for inheritance and polymorphic deletion doesn't need a virtual destructor and paying for the vtable overhead would be unnecessary.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct Base { virtual ~Base() = default; virtual void speak() { } };\nstruct Derived : Base { std::vector<int> data; void speak() override { } };\n\nBase* b = new Derived();\ndelete b;  // With a virtual destructor: Derived's destructor runs, 'data' is properly freed.\n           // Without it: only Base's destructor would run - undefined behavior, and a leak.\n```",
