    experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
    difficulty: "MEDIUM",
    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "The Rule of Three says that if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three, because needing one usua...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the C++ concept and can apply it correctly in production-oriented code.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise explanation of the underlying rule.\n- They want you to identify the practical trade-off or edge case that commonly causes mistakes.\n- They want a concrete example showing how the concept is used correctly.",
    sampleAnswer: "The Rule of Three says that if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three, because needing one usually means the class manages a resource that the compiler's default member-wise copy/destroy logic would handle incorrectly (like a raw owned pointer). The Rule of Five extends this for C++11 and later: if you need any of those three, you should also consider defining a move constructor and move assignment operator, since without them the compiler either won't generate move operations at all (falling back to copying, which is correct but misses a performance opportunity) or, in some cases, won't generate them because a user-declared destructor is present, silently disabling moves. In modern C++, many classes should instead follow the 'Rule of Zero' - hold resources exclusively through RAII types like unique_ptr/vector/string that already manage the five operations correctly, so the class itself needs to declare none of them.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe Rule of Three says that if a class needs a user-defined destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator, it almost certainly needs all three, because needing one usually means the class manages a resource that the compiler's default member-wise copy/destroy logic would handle incorrectly (like a raw owned pointer). The Rule of Five extends this for C++11 and later: if you need any of those three, you should also consider defining a move constructor and move assignment operator, since without them the compiler either won't generate move operations at all (falling back to copying, which is correct but misses a performance opportunity) or, in some cases, won't generate them because a user-declared destructor is present, silently disabling moves. In modern C++, many classes should instead follow the 'Rule of Zero' - hold resources exclusively through RAII types like unique_ptr/vector/string that already manage the five operations correctly, so the class itself needs to declare none of them.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nclass Buffer {\n    int* data; size_t size;\npublic:\n    Buffer(size_t n) : data(new int[n]), size(n) {}\n    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }\n    Buffer(const Buffer& other);              // copy constructor\n    Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other);    // copy assignment\n    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept;           // move constructor\n    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept; // move assignment\n};\n// A 'Rule of Zero' alternative would replace the raw pointer with std::vector<int>\n// or std::unique_ptr<int[]>, needing none of these five declared manually.\n```",
    keyPoints: ["They want a precise explanation of the underlying rule.", "They want you to identify the practical trade-off or edge case that commonly causes mistakes.", "They want a concrete example showing how the concept is used correctly."],
