    shortDescription: "A virtual function is a member function that a derived class can override, and a call to it through a base-class pointer or reference resolves at runtime to the override matching t...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "A virtual function is a member function that a derived class can override, and a call to it through a base-class pointer or reference resolves at runtime to the override matching the object's actual (dynamic) type, rather than the static type of the pointer/reference used to call it. The language specifies this dispatch behavior; it's commonly implemented by compilers using a vtable (a per-class table of function pointers) and a hidden vptr member added to each polymorphic object, though the standard doesn't mandate this specific implementation. This is the standard C++ mechanism for runtime polymorphism - writing code against a base-class interface that correctly invokes derived-class-specific behavior without the caller needing to know the concrete type.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA virtual function is a member function that a derived class can override, and a call to it through a base-class pointer or reference resolves at runtime to the override matching the object's actual (dynamic) type, rather than the static type of the pointer/reference used to call it. The language specifies this dispatch behavior; it's commonly implemented by compilers using a vtable (a per-class table of function pointers) and a hidden vptr member added to each polymorphic object, though the standard doesn't mandate this specific implementation. This is the standard C++ mechanism for runtime polymorphism - writing code against a base-class interface that correctly invokes derived-class-specific behavior without the caller needing to know the concrete type.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct Base { virtual void speak() { std::cout << \"Base\"; } };\nstruct Derived : Base { void speak() override { std::cout << \"Derived\"; } };\n\nBase& b = derivedInstance;\nb.speak(); // prints \"Derived\" - resolved at runtime based on the object's actual type\n```",
