    shortDescription: "Virtual inheritance addresses the 'diamond problem': if class D inherits from both B and C, and both B and C inherit from a common base A, then without virtual inheritance D ends u...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "Virtual inheritance addresses the 'diamond problem': if class D inherits from both B and C, and both B and C inherit from a common base A, then without virtual inheritance D ends up containing two separate subobjects of A (one via B, one via C), which is ambiguous and wasteful when only one logical A is intended. Declaring the inheritance from A as virtual in both B and C ensures D contains exactly one shared A subobject, at the cost of a somewhat more complex and slightly slower object layout (an extra level of indirection to locate the shared base), and the requirement that the most-derived class is responsible for initializing the virtual base directly, bypassing the intermediate classes' own initialization of it.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nVirtual inheritance addresses the 'diamond problem': if class D inherits from both B and C, and both B and C inherit from a common base A, then without virtual inheritance D ends up containing two separate subobjects of A (one via B, one via C), which is ambiguous and wasteful when only one logical A is intended. Declaring the inheritance from A as virtual in both B and C ensures D contains exactly one shared A subobject, at the cost of a somewhat more complex and slightly slower object layout (an extra level of indirection to locate the shared base), and the requirement that the most-derived class is responsible for initializing the virtual base directly, bypassing the intermediate classes' own initialization of it.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct A { int value; };\nstruct B : virtual A { };\nstruct C : virtual A { };\nstruct D : B, C { };  // D has exactly one A subobject, not two, because of virtual inheritance\n```",
