    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "While the C++ standard only specifies the observable behavior of virtual dispatch and not its implementation, most compilers implement it with a vtable - a static, per-class array of function pointers, one entry per virtual function, pointing to the correct override for that class - and a vptr, a hidden pointer added to each object of a polymorphic class, set during construction to point to its class's vtable. A virtual call through a base pointer follows the object's vptr to its actual class's vtable and calls the function pointer at the appropriate slot, which is what allows the same call syntax to invoke different code depending on the object's dynamic type, at the cost of one extra pointer indirection compared to a non-virtual (statically resolved) call.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nWhile the C++ standard only specifies the observable behavior of virtual dispatch and not its implementation, most compilers implement it with a vtable - a static, per-class array of function pointers, one entry per virtual function, pointing to the correct override for that class - and a vptr, a hidden pointer added to each object of a polymorphic class, set during construction to point to its class's vtable. A virtual call through a base pointer follows the object's vptr to its actual class's vtable and calls the function pointer at the appropriate slot, which is what allows the same call syntax to invoke different code depending on the object's dynamic type, at the cost of one extra pointer indirection compared to a non-virtual (statically resolved) call.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Conceptual layout for: struct Base { virtual void f(); virtual void g(); };\n//                        struct Derived : Base { void f() override; };\n// Derived object layout: [ vptr -> Derived's vtable ] [ Base's other data members ] ...\n// Derived's vtable: [ slot 0: Derived::f ] [ slot 1: Base::g (inherited, not overridden) ]\n```",
