    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "A base class intended for polymorphic use needs its destructor to be virtual so deletion through a base pointer runs the correct derived destructor, as covered earlier - but declar...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "A base class intended for polymorphic use needs its destructor to be virtual so deletion through a base pointer runs the correct derived destructor, as covered earlier - but declaring any destructor (even `~Base() = default;`) is still a user declaration for Rule-of-Five purposes, which by itself suppresses the compiler's automatically-generated move constructor and move assignment operator (though copy operations are still generated, just with a deprecation warning in some compilers). If a base class needs to remain movable, the move operations may need to be explicitly defaulted alongside the virtual destructor, or the class should rely on Rule-of-Zero design so it never needs a user-declared destructor in the first place.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA base class intended for polymorphic use needs its destructor to be virtual so deletion through a base pointer runs the correct derived destructor, as covered earlier - but declaring any destructor (even `~Base() = default;`) is still a user declaration for Rule-of-Five purposes, which by itself suppresses the compiler's automatically-generated move constructor and move assignment operator (though copy operations are still generated, just with a deprecation warning in some compilers). If a base class needs to remain movable, the move operations may need to be explicitly defaulted alongside the virtual destructor, or the class should rely on Rule-of-Zero design so it never needs a user-declared destructor in the first place.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct Base {\n    virtual ~Base() = default;         // needed for correct polymorphic deletion\n    Base(Base&&) = default;             // must be explicitly restored:\n    Base& operator=(Base&&) = default;  // declaring ~Base() suppressed the implicit ones\n};\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nVALUE CATEGORIES & MOVE SEMANTICS\n\n==============================================================================\n```",
