    shortDescription: "An lvalue refers to an object that has an identifiable, persistent location in memory and can generally appear on the left side of an assignment (a named variable, a dereferenced p...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand C++ value categories, resource transfer, and the mechanisms behind efficient object passing.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.\n- They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.\n- They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself.",
    sampleAnswer: "An lvalue refers to an object that has an identifiable, persistent location in memory and can generally appear on the left side of an assignment (a named variable, a dereferenced pointer, an element accessed via subscript). An rvalue is typically a temporary value without a persistent, addressable identity beyond the current expression (a literal, the result of most non-reference-returning function calls, an arithmetic expression's result). This distinction is what move semantics is built on: an rvalue reference (T&&) can bind to an rvalue specifically because it's safe to 'steal' resources from something about to be discarded anyway, which wouldn't be safe to do to an lvalue that the rest of the program might still use.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nAn lvalue refers to an object that has an identifiable, persistent location in memory and can generally appear on the left side of an assignment (a named variable, a dereferenced pointer, an element accessed via subscript). An rvalue is typically a temporary value without a persistent, addressable identity beyond the current expression (a literal, the result of most non-reference-returning function calls, an arithmetic expression's result). This distinction is what move semantics is built on: an rvalue reference (T&&) can bind to an rvalue specifically because it's safe to 'steal' resources from something about to be discarded anyway, which wouldn't be safe to do to an lvalue that the rest of the program might still use.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nint x = 5;      // x is an lvalue; 5 is an rvalue (a literal)\nint y = x + 1;  // (x + 1) is an rvalue - a temporary with no persistent address\nint& refX = x;      // OK: lvalue reference binds to an lvalue\nint&& refTemp = x + 1; // OK: rvalue reference binds to an rvalue temporary\n```",
