    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "No - std::move performs no operation on the object itself; it's purely a cast that converts its argument to an rvalue reference (technically std::remove_reference<T>::type&&), whic...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand C++ value categories, resource transfer, and the mechanisms behind efficient object passing.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.\n- They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.\n- They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself.",
    sampleAnswer: "No - std::move performs no operation on the object itself; it's purely a cast that converts its argument to an rvalue reference (technically std::remove_reference<T>::type&&), which signals to the compiler and to overload resolution that this object is eligible to be moved from. Whether an actual move happens at all depends on what the resulting rvalue reference is subsequently used for: if it's passed to a move constructor or move assignment operator, that function's implementation is what actually transfers the resource. If nothing consumes the moved-from rvalue reference, or the type has no move constructor and falls back to copying, std::move has effectively done nothing productive, and the object it was applied to should still be treated as being in a valid but unspecified state afterward regardless.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nNo - std::move performs no operation on the object itself; it's purely a cast that converts its argument to an rvalue reference (technically std::remove_reference<T>::type&&), which signals to the compiler and to overload resolution that this object is eligible to be moved from. Whether an actual move happens at all depends on what the resulting rvalue reference is subsequently used for: if it's passed to a move constructor or move assignment operator, that function's implementation is what actually transfers the resource. If nothing consumes the moved-from rvalue reference, or the type has no move constructor and falls back to copying, std::move has effectively done nothing productive, and the object it was applied to should still be treated as being in a valid but unspecified state afterward regardless.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::string a = \"hello\";\nstd::string b = std::move(a);\n// std::move(a) itself just casts 'a' to an rvalue reference.\n// The actual transfer of a's internal buffer to b happens inside std::string's\n// move constructor, which is selected because of that cast.\n// 'a' is now in a valid but unspecified state - safe to reassign, unsafe to assume empty.\n```",
