    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nCalled through a base pointer/reference after construction is complete, a virtual function correctly dispatches to the derived override, as expected. But if a virtual function is called from within the base class's own constructor (or destructor), dynamic dispatch does not reach the derived override - the object's dynamic type is treated as the base class during its own construction, because the derived part of the object hasn't been constructed yet (and, symmetrically, is already destroyed during base destruction), so the base class's own version of the function runs instead, which surprises many C++ programmers coming from languages where this isn't the case.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct Base {\n    Base() { init(); }              // calls Base::init(), NOT Derived::init()\n    virtual void init() { std::cout << \"Base::init\\n\"; }\n};\nstruct Derived : Base {\n    void init() override { std::cout << \"Derived::init\\n\"; }\n};\nint main() { Derived d; }\n// Output: Base::init  (not Derived::init, even though d is a Derived object)\n```",
