    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nconst int* p means p points to data that must be treated as const through p - you can reassign p to point elsewhere, but can't modify *p through p. int* const p means p itself is const - it must always point to the same address, but the data it points to can be modified through it (unless that data is separately const). Reading these declarations right-to-left from the const keyword's position is the standard trick: const int* reads as 'pointer to const int,' while int* const reads as 'const pointer to int.'\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nint x = 5, y = 10;\nconst int* p1 = &x;\np1 = &y;        // OK: p1 can be reassigned\n// *p1 = 20;    // ERROR: can't modify *p1 through a pointer-to-const\n\nint* const p2 = &x;\n*p2 = 20;       // OK: the pointee can be modified\n// p2 = &y;     // ERROR: p2 itself is const, can't be reassigned\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nTEMPLATES & GENERIC PROGRAMMING\n\n==============================================================================\n```",
