    sampleAnswer: "A function template lets you write a single generic definition parameterized by one or more types (or, with C++20, constrained by concepts), and the compiler generates ('instantiates') a concrete function for each distinct set of template arguments actually used in the program, at compile time. This gives the performance of a hand-written type-specific function (no runtime type dispatch overhead) with the reusability of writing the logic once, at the cost of longer compile times and potential code bloat if the same template is instantiated for many distinct types.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA function template lets you write a single generic definition parameterized by one or more types (or, with C++20, constrained by concepts), and the compiler generates ('instantiates') a concrete function for each distinct set of template arguments actually used in the program, at compile time. This gives the performance of a hand-written type-specific function (no runtime type dispatch overhead) with the reusability of writing the logic once, at the cost of longer compile times and potential code bloat if the same template is instantiated for many distinct types.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T>\nT maxOf(T a, T b) { return (a > b) ? a : b; }\n\nmaxOf(3, 7);       // instantiates maxOf<int>\nmaxOf(3.5, 2.1);   // instantiates maxOf<double>, a separate generated function\n```",
