    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Separate overloads for each type duplicate the same logic by hand and don't scale to types the author didn't anticipate; templates express the logic once and let the compiler gener...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "Separate overloads for each type duplicate the same logic by hand and don't scale to types the author didn't anticipate; templates express the logic once and let the compiler generate the type-specific versions automatically, including for types defined later by other code (like user-defined classes), as long as those types support the operations the template uses. Compared to runtime polymorphism (virtual functions), templates resolve which code to run at compile time rather than runtime, avoiding virtual-call overhead and enabling more aggressive compiler optimization (since the concrete type is known at each call site), at the cost of needing the type to be known at compile time and potentially larger binaries from multiple instantiations.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nSeparate overloads for each type duplicate the same logic by hand and don't scale to types the author didn't anticipate; templates express the logic once and let the compiler generate the type-specific versions automatically, including for types defined later by other code (like user-defined classes), as long as those types support the operations the template uses. Compared to runtime polymorphism (virtual functions), templates resolve which code to run at compile time rather than runtime, avoiding virtual-call overhead and enabling more aggressive compiler optimization (since the concrete type is known at each call site), at the cost of needing the type to be known at compile time and potentially larger binaries from multiple instantiations.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Runtime polymorphism: one function, dynamic dispatch cost, needs a common base class\nvoid render(Shape* s) { s->draw(); }\n\n// Template: compiler generates a separate, directly-callable version per type,\n// with no virtual-call overhead and no requirement that types share a base class\ntemplate <typename T> void render(const T& shape) { shape.draw(); }\n```",
