    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to evaluate whether you can reason about compile-time generic programming and type deduction instead of treating templates as simple text substitution.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain when code is instantiated or types are deduced.\n- They want you to distinguish similar mechanisms such as auto, decltype, specialization, and runtime polymorphism.\n- They want a practical example showing why the choice matters in maintainable C++ code.",
    sampleAnswer: "Full specialization provides a completely separate implementation for one specific, fully-determined combination of template arguments, overriding the generic template entirely for that exact case. Partial specialization (available for class templates, not function templates, which instead use overloading for a similar effect) provides a separate implementation that still has some template parameters left generic, matching a pattern of arguments rather than one exact combination - most commonly used to specialize a template differently for pointer types, or for a fixed number of parameters, while still leaving the underlying type generic.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFull specialization provides a completely separate implementation for one specific, fully-determined combination of template arguments, overriding the generic template entirely for that exact case. Partial specialization (available for class templates, not function templates, which instead use overloading for a similar effect) provides a separate implementation that still has some template parameters left generic, matching a pattern of arguments rather than one exact combination - most commonly used to specialize a template differently for pointer types, or for a fixed number of parameters, while still leaving the underlying type generic.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T> struct Container { /* generic version */ };\n\ntemplate <> struct Container<bool> { /* full specialization: entirely custom for bool */ };\n\ntemplate <typename T> struct Container<T*> { /* partial specialization: any pointer type */ };\n```",
