    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Type deduction lets the compiler infer a type from context rather than requiring it to be spelled out explicitly - most visibly with auto for variable declarations and function ret...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to evaluate whether you can reason about compile-time generic programming and type deduction instead of treating templates as simple text substitution.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain when code is instantiated or types are deduced.\n- They want you to distinguish similar mechanisms such as auto, decltype, specialization, and runtime polymorphism.\n- They want a practical example showing why the choice matters in maintainable C++ code.",
    sampleAnswer: "Type deduction lets the compiler infer a type from context rather than requiring it to be spelled out explicitly - most visibly with auto for variable declarations and function return types, and with template argument deduction for function templates. auto deduces a type the same way template argument deduction does, generally dropping top-level const/reference qualifiers unless explicitly requested (auto&, const auto&), and is evaluated from the initializer's value. decltype instead inspects the exact declared type of an expression, including references and const-ness, without evaluating it, which makes it useful when you need to preserve a type's exact form (e.g., writing a function template whose return type should exactly match an expression involving its parameters).",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nType deduction lets the compiler infer a type from context rather than requiring it to be spelled out explicitly - most visibly with auto for variable declarations and function return types, and with template argument deduction for function templates. auto deduces a type the same way template argument deduction does, generally dropping top-level const/reference qualifiers unless explicitly requested (auto&, const auto&), and is evaluated from the initializer's value. decltype instead inspects the exact declared type of an expression, including references and const-ness, without evaluating it, which makes it useful when you need to preserve a type's exact form (e.g., writing a function template whose return type should exactly match an expression involving its parameters).\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nconst int& getRef();\nauto a = getRef();        // a is deduced as 'int' - top-level const/reference stripped\ndecltype(getRef()) b = getRef();  // b is 'const int&' - decltype preserves the exact type\n```",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to explain when code is instantiated or types are deduced.", "They want you to distinguish similar mechanisms such as auto, decltype, specialization, and runtime polymorphism.", "They want a practical example showing why the choice matters in maintainable C++ code."],
