    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Prefer templates when the set of types is known and fixed at compile time, performance is critical enough that virtual-call overhead and lack of inlining matter, and the types invo...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you understand both the language semantics and the design consequences of runtime polymorphism.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.\n- They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.\n- They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case.",
    sampleAnswer: "Prefer templates when the set of types is known and fixed at compile time, performance is critical enough that virtual-call overhead and lack of inlining matter, and the types involved don't need to share a common base class (duck typing based purely on supported operations, as with STL algorithms operating over any iterator type). Prefer runtime polymorphism when the concrete type genuinely isn't known until runtime (plugins, heterogeneous collections of objects processed uniformly, a factory returning different subclasses based on runtime input), when binary size matters more than raw call overhead (templates can bloat code size with many instantiations), or when the interface needs to be usable across a dynamically-loaded library boundary, where template instantiation visibility becomes awkward.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nPrefer templates when the set of types is known and fixed at compile time, performance is critical enough that virtual-call overhead and lack of inlining matter, and the types involved don't need to share a common base class (duck typing based purely on supported operations, as with STL algorithms operating over any iterator type). Prefer runtime polymorphism when the concrete type genuinely isn't known until runtime (plugins, heterogeneous collections of objects processed uniformly, a factory returning different subclasses based on runtime input), when binary size matters more than raw call overhead (templates can bloat code size with many instantiations), or when the interface needs to be usable across a dynamically-loaded library boundary, where template instantiation visibility becomes awkward.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\n// Templates: type known at compile time, used generically without inheritance\nstd::sort(container.begin(), container.end());  // works on any container's iterators\n\n// Virtual functions: type only known at runtime, from user input or a plugin\nstd::unique_ptr<Shape> s = ShapeFactory::create(userSelectedType);\ns->draw();  // which draw() runs depends on runtime input, not compile-time knowledge\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nSTL: CONTAINERS & ALGORITHMS\n\n==============================================================================\n```",
    keyPoints: ["They want you to explain dispatch and object lifetime precisely.", "They want you to recognize the relevant inheritance or virtual-function rule and its trade-offs.", "They want you to connect the concept to a practical class hierarchy or runtime use case."],
