    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\npush_back takes an already-constructed object (or something implicitly convertible to one) and copies or moves it into the container's storage. emplace_back instead forwards its arguments directly to the element type's constructor, constructing the new element in place within the container's storage, which avoids an extra temporary object and a subsequent copy/move for types constructed from multiple arguments. For a type with a cheap or already-existing object to insert, the difference is negligible or nonexistent (particularly with move semantics); the benefit of emplace_back is clearest when constructing a new object directly from constructor arguments.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<std::pair<int, std::string>> v;\nv.push_back(std::make_pair(1, \"a\"));  // constructs a temporary pair, then moves it in\nv.emplace_back(1, \"a\");                // constructs the pair directly in place, no temporary\n```",
