    sampleAnswer: "Both perform binary search on a sorted range and run in O(log n) comparisons (though O(n) if used on a non-random-access iterator range, since advancing the iterator itself becomes linear). std::lower_bound returns an iterator to the first element not less than (i.e., >=) the given value - the earliest valid insertion point that keeps the range sorted while placing the new element before any equal elements. std::upper_bound returns an iterator to the first element strictly greater than the given value - the insertion point after any equal elements. Together, [lower_bound, upper_bound) delimits exactly the range of elements equal to the given value, which is how std::equal_range is implemented.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBoth perform binary search on a sorted range and run in O(log n) comparisons (though O(n) if used on a non-random-access iterator range, since advancing the iterator itself becomes linear). std::lower_bound returns an iterator to the first element not less than (i.e., >=) the given value - the earliest valid insertion point that keeps the range sorted while placing the new element before any equal elements. std::upper_bound returns an iterator to the first element strictly greater than the given value - the insertion point after any equal elements. Together, [lower_bound, upper_bound) delimits exactly the range of elements equal to the given value, which is how std::equal_range is implemented.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 2, 2, 5};\nauto lo = std::lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 2);  // points to first '2' (index 1)\nauto hi = std::upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 2);  // points to '5' (index 4)\n// hi - lo == 3, the count of elements equal to 2\n```",
