    sampleAnswer: "A hash-set-based approach (std::unordered_set) gives expected O(n) time and O(n) extra space: iterate once, and if an element is already present in the set when you try to insert it, it's a duplicate. A sort-based approach (std::sort followed by a linear scan with std::adjacent_find, or comparing each element to the previous one) gives O(n log n) time but O(1) extra space (aside from sorting itself, which can be in-place), and also has the side effect of leaving the data sorted, which may or may not be desirable. The hash-based approach is generally preferred when extra memory is acceptable and preserving the original order matters; the sort-based approach is preferred when memory is constrained or the data needs to be sorted anyway.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA hash-set-based approach (std::unordered_set) gives expected O(n) time and O(n) extra space: iterate once, and if an element is already present in the set when you try to insert it, it's a duplicate. A sort-based approach (std::sort followed by a linear scan with std::adjacent_find, or comparing each element to the previous one) gives O(n log n) time but O(1) extra space (aside from sorting itself, which can be in-place), and also has the side effect of leaving the data sorted, which may or may not be desirable. The hash-based approach is generally preferred when extra memory is acceptable and preserving the original order matters; the sort-based approach is preferred when memory is constrained or the data needs to be sorted anyway.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<int> v = {4, 2, 5, 2, 8, 4};\nstd::unordered_set<int> seen;\nfor (int x : v) {\n    if (!seen.insert(x).second) {\n        std::cout << x << \" is a duplicate\\n\";  // insert().second is false if already present\n    }\n}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nPOINTERS & CODING PROBLEMS\n\n==============================================================================\n```",
