    shortDescription: "A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address, can be reassigned to point to different objects over its lifetime, can be null, and requires explicit dereferencing (*p) to ac...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand C++ value categories, resource transfer, and the mechanisms behind efficient object passing.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want a precise distinction between lvalues, rvalues, references, and moving.\n- They want you to explain what actually happens to the source object and why copying may be avoided.\n- They want a practical example that uses the feature correctly rather than treating std::move as an operation that moves by itself.",
    sampleAnswer: "A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address, can be reassigned to point to different objects over its lifetime, can be null, and requires explicit dereferencing (*p) to access the pointed-to object. A reference is an alias for an existing object established at initialization, cannot be reassigned to refer to a different object afterward, cannot be null (a reference must be bound to a valid object when declared, at least in well-defined code - a dangling reference from a destroyed object is possible but is itself undefined behavior to use), and is used with the same syntax as the object itself, no explicit dereference needed. References are generally preferred for function parameters when null isn't a meaningful state and reseating isn't needed, since they're syntactically simpler and their non-null invariant makes calling code slightly safer to reason about.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA pointer is a variable that stores a memory address, can be reassigned to point to different objects over its lifetime, can be null, and requires explicit dereferencing (*p) to access the pointed-to object. A reference is an alias for an existing object established at initialization, cannot be reassigned to refer to a different object afterward, cannot be null (a reference must be bound to a valid object when declared, at least in well-defined code - a dangling reference from a destroyed object is possible but is itself undefined behavior to use), and is used with the same syntax as the object itself, no explicit dereference needed. References are generally preferred for function parameters when null isn't a meaningful state and reseating isn't needed, since they're syntactically simpler and their non-null invariant makes calling code slightly safer to reason about.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nint x = 10;\nint* p = &x;   // p holds x's address; can be reassigned: p = &otherVar;\nint& r = x;    // r is an alias for x; r = otherVar; would assign otherVar's VALUE to x, not reseat r\n*p = 20;       // modifies x through the pointer\nr = 30;        // modifies x directly through the reference\n```",
