    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to test whether you can reason about addresses, ownership, indirection, and data structures at a low level.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want you to explain what is stored and how the operations change the data structure.\n- They want you to identify lifetime, ownership, and safety considerations.\n- They want a working example with correct pointer manipulation and no hidden undefined behavior.",
    sampleAnswer: "A hand-rolled dynamic array needs to correctly implement the Rule of Five (or Rule of Three at minimum): a destructor releasing the allocated buffer, a copy constructor and copy assignment operator that perform a deep copy (not copying the raw pointer, which would cause a double-free), and ideally move operations to avoid unnecessary deep copies when the source is a temporary. It should also handle self-assignment safely in operator=, use bounds-checked access (or clearly document that operator[] is unchecked while at() throws, matching std::vector's convention), and correctly manage the distinction between size (elements in use) and capacity (allocated slots) if it needs to support efficient growth, similar to std::vector's amortized-O(1) push_back.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nA hand-rolled dynamic array needs to correctly implement the Rule of Five (or Rule of Three at minimum): a destructor releasing the allocated buffer, a copy constructor and copy assignment operator that perform a deep copy (not copying the raw pointer, which would cause a double-free), and ideally move operations to avoid unnecessary deep copies when the source is a temporary. It should also handle self-assignment safely in operator=, use bounds-checked access (or clearly document that operator[] is unchecked while at() throws, matching std::vector's convention), and correctly manage the distinction between size (elements in use) and capacity (allocated slots) if it needs to support efficient growth, similar to std::vector's amortized-O(1) push_back.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nclass Array {\n    int* data; size_t size_;\npublic:\n    explicit Array(size_t n) : data(new int[n]()), size_(n) {}\n    ~Array() { delete[] data; }\n    Array(const Array& other) : data(new int[other.size_]), size_(other.size_) {\n        std::copy(other.data, other.data + size_, data);\n    }\n    Array& operator=(const Array& other) {\n        if (this == &other) return *this;\n        int* newData = new int[other.size_];\n        std::copy(other.data, other.data + other.size_, newData);\n        delete[] data;\n        data = newData; size_ = other.size_;\n        return *this;\n    }\n    int& operator[](size_t i) { return data[i]; }\n    size_t size() const { return size_; }\n};\n```",
