    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "The standard iterative approach walks the list once, re-pointing each node's next to the previous node as it goes, in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. With raw pointers, reversing i...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you can choose and use standard-library containers and algorithms based on their real complexity, memory behavior, and semantics.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- They want the relevant container or algorithm guarantees explained accurately.\n- They want you to discuss complexity and practical trade-offs such as cache locality or iterator behavior.\n- They want you to choose the STL tool that fits the actual workload rather than relying on a blanket rule.",
    sampleAnswer: "The standard iterative approach walks the list once, re-pointing each node's next to the previous node as it goes, in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. With raw pointers, reversing is purely a matter of re-linking - no allocation or deallocation is needed, and the caller retains responsibility for eventually deleting all nodes. If the list is instead built from std::unique_ptr<Node> for owned links, reversing is more involved because a unique_ptr can't simply be reassigned to point elsewhere without transferring ownership explicitly (std::move), and naive re-linking can accidentally destroy nodes prematurely if ownership isn't moved correctly at each step - which is worth mentioning explicitly if the interviewer specifies smart-pointer-based nodes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe standard iterative approach walks the list once, re-pointing each node's next to the previous node as it goes, in O(n) time and O(1) extra space. With raw pointers, reversing is purely a matter of re-linking - no allocation or deallocation is needed, and the caller retains responsibility for eventually deleting all nodes. If the list is instead built from std::unique_ptr<Node> for owned links, reversing is more involved because a unique_ptr can't simply be reassigned to point elsewhere without transferring ownership explicitly (std::move), and naive re-linking can accidentally destroy nodes prematurely if ownership isn't moved correctly at each step - which is worth mentioning explicitly if the interviewer specifies smart-pointer-based nodes.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstruct Node { int val; Node* next; };\n\nNode* reverse(Node* head) {\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    Node* current = head;\n    while (current) {\n        Node* next = current->next;\n        current->next = prev;\n        prev = current;\n        current = next;\n    }\n    return prev;  // new head\n}\n```",
